    <p class="title is-5 mathjax">
      
        Diffusion Learning with Partial <span class="search-hit mathjax">Agent</span> Participation and Local Updates
      
    </p>
    <p class="authors">
      <span class="has-text-black-bis has-text-weight-semibold">Authors:</span>
      
      <a href="/search/?searchtype=author&amp;query=Rizk%2C+E">Elsa Rizk</a>, 
      
      <a href="/search/?searchtype=author&amp;query=Yuan%2C+K">Kun Yuan</a>, 
      
      <a href="/search/?searchtype=author&amp;query=Sayed%2C+A+H">Ali H. Sayed</a>
      
    </p>
    
    <p class="abstract mathjax">
      <span class="has-text-black-bis has-text-weight-semibold">Abstract</span>:
      <span class="abstract-short has-text-grey-dark mathjax" id="2505.11307v1-abstract-short" style="display: inline;">
        Diffusion learning is a framework that endows edge devices with advanced intelligence. By processing and analyzing <span class="search-hit mathjax">data</span>&hellip;
        <a class="is-size-7" style="white-space: nowrap;" onclick="document.getElementById('2505.11307v1-abstract-full').style.display = 'inline'; document.getElementById('2505.11307v1-abstract-short').style.display = 'none';">&#9661; More</a>
      </span>
      <span class="abstract-full has-text-grey-dark mathjax" id="2505.11307v1-abstract-full" style="display: none;">
        Diffusion learning is a framework that endows edge devices with advanced intelligence. By processing and analyzing <span class="search-hit mathjax">data</span> <span class="search-hit mathjax">locally</span> and allowing each <span class="search-hit mathjax">agent</span> to communicate with its immediate neighbors, diffusion effectively protects the <span class="search-hit mathjax">privacy</span> of edge devices, enables real-time response, and reduces reliance on central servers. However, traditional diffusion learning relies on communication at every iteration, leading to communication overhead, especially with large learning models. Furthermore, the inherent volatility of edge devices, stemming from power outages or signal loss, poses challenges to reliable communication between neighboring <span class="search-hit mathjax">agents</span>. To mitigate these issues, this paper investigates an enhanced diffusion learning approach incorporating local updates and partial <span class="search-hit mathjax">agent</span> participation. Local updates will curtail communication frequency, while partial <span class="search-hit mathjax">agent</span> participation will allow for the inclusion of <span class="search-hit mathjax">agents</span> based on their availability. We prove that the resulting algorithm is stable in the mean-square error sense and provide a tight analysis of its Mean-Square-Deviation (MSD) performance. Various numerical experiments are conducted to illustrate our theoretical findings.
        <a class="is-size-7" style="white-space: nowrap;" onclick="document.getElementById('2505.11307v1-abstract-full').style.display = 'none'; document.getElementById('2505.11307v1-abstract-short').style.display = 'inline';">&#9651; Less</a>
      </span>
    </p>
    

    <p class="is-size-7"><span class="has-text-black-bis has-text-weight-semibold">Submitted</span> 16 May, 2025; 
      <span class="has-text-black-bis has-text-weight-semibold">originally announced</span> May 2025.
      
    </p>
    
